# === The level carrier ===
#
# The per-level Russell-Whitehead carrier per spec §3.1 Read A.
# The substrate-altitude carrier for *a single Bateson level* —
# the type-stratification slice at a fixed level index N. Routes
# through `@epistemologic/math/russell-whitehead` for the
# stratification operator at the foundation altitude; declared
# HERE as a typed carrier at the bateson_learning altitude so
# consumer shards can name a Bateson level without pulling the
# full Russell-Whitehead machinery.
#
# Identity contract: byte-equality on the underlying ref. Two
# levels with byte-equal underlying refs ARE the same level under
# content-addressing — by construction, since a level's OID IS
# the Russell-Whitehead level index at the path-syntax altitude.
# Same shape as viable.identity, autopoiesis.organization, and
# distinction's mark carrier.
type level = ref

# === The HierarchyTop carrier ===
#
# T_reg per spec §5 Read B (the natural form). The substrate-
# altitude carrier for *the whole Russell-Whitehead-stratified
# universe ⊕_N V_N viewed as a graded carrier*. Routes through
# `@epistemologic/math/kac-graded` for the graded-algebra machinery
# at the foundation altitude and `@epistemologic/math/russell-
# whitehead` for the type-stratification at the path-syntax
# altitude; declared HERE as a typed carrier at the
# bateson_learning altitude so consumer shards can name the whole
# hierarchy without re-declaring the graded structure.
#
# Per recognition #51 (mirror IS an expanding Hilbert space) +
# §8.3 ratified: hierarchy_top's underlying ref IS the Hilbert
# space whose dimension expands with each Bateson lift. The
# substrate's content-addressing exhibits the expansion at
# construction time — a new substrate-pull recognition extends
# the Hilbert space by one Russell-Whitehead level; the OID layer
# carries the level index.
#
# Identity contract: byte-equality on the underlying ref. Same
# shape discipline as autopoiesis.organization.
type hierarchy_top = ref

# === The HierarchyBase carrier ===
#
# T_regd per spec §5 Read B. The substrate-altitude carrier for
# *the same universe viewed from the level-0 floor*. A graded
# carrier reading sub-N components; the verdict surfaces against
# the substrate's existing `transparency<level>` family per spec
# §6.7. Routes through the same Kac graded-algebra machinery as
# hierarchy_top; declared as a distinct type to preserve the
# (T_reg, T_regd) parametric-carrier discipline at the species'
# boundary.
#
# Per [[feedback-no-bare-types]] hierarchy_base carries the
# level-floor anchor alongside the graded ref; a consumer reading
# the base view can rank without losing floor-of-which-the-base-
# is-a-view.
#
# Identity contract: byte-equality on (floor_level, graded_view).
# Two hierarchy_base records ARE the same view under content-
# addressing iff both carriers are byte-equal.
type hierarchy_base = {
  floor_level:  level,
  graded_view:  ref,
}

# === The bateson_system carrier ===
#
# The substrate-altitude lift of a system carrying Bateson
# logical-type discipline as a typed record. Each field carries
# the species-specific data at the bateson_learning altitude:
#
# - `hierarchy`        the system's whole Russell-Whitehead-
#                      stratified universe; T_reg for Read B.
#                      The Hilbert-space expansion witness at
#                      construction time (per recognition #51).
# - `floor`            the level-0 view of the same hierarchy;
#                      T_regd for Read B. The base case the
#                      Russell-Whitehead lift starts from.
# - `top_level`        the highest level currently inhabited by
#                      the system; the empirical ceiling carrier
#                      (Read C question parameterised). The
#                      substrate admits arbitrary N structurally;
#                      a specific system has a specific top_level
#                      at a specific tick.
# - `lift_map`         the Russell-Whitehead lift operator
#                      between adjacent levels; an action ref the
#                      consumer realizes per species. The species
#                      is bateson_learning iff this map closes
#                      the level-N → level-N+1 transition
#                      gauge-equivalently to the graded
#                      representation.
# - `gauge_group`      the family-root gauge group whose graded
#                      representation acts on the hierarchy.
#                      Inherited from coherence-parametric's
#                      lock_pair carrier; named here as the
#                      species' instantiation.
#
# Identity contract: byte-equality on (hierarchy, floor,
# top_level, lift_map, gauge_group). Two bateson_system records
# ARE the same system under content-addressing iff all five
# carriers are byte-equal. Same shape discipline as
# coherence-parametric.lock_pair, viable.viable_system, and
# autopoiesis.autopoietic_system.
type bateson_system = {
  hierarchy:    hierarchy_top,
  floor:        hierarchy_base,
  top_level:    level,
  lift_map:     ref,
  gauge_group:  ref,
}

# === type_lift — the natural substitution τ : level(N) ↔ level(N+1) (Read A) ===
#
# Spec §3.1 Read A: the per-level Russell-Whitehead lift. The
# substitution is natural because Russell-Whitehead 1910's
# stratification operator (Principia Mathematica Vol. I, §II
# "The Theory of Logical Types") says: the inclusion V_N ↪ V_{N+1}
# is the canonical inclusion of a type-level into the next.
#
# `type_lift(n)` is the τ-action under Read A: given a level `n`,
# returns the level one Russell-Whitehead step up. The
# substitution's naturality is substrate-evident through the
# `in @<altitude>` wrapping at the path-syntax altitude — each
# `in @<x>` IS a type_lift instance at the substrate altitude.
#
# Body discharges per-species at the realisation boundary. Same
# shape as the parametric carrier's measurement actions and the
# viable shard's `stability_of` / autopoiesis shard's `produce`
# actions.
type_lift(n: level) -> level { \ }

# === graded_act — the canonical ρ action τ : HierarchyTop ↔ HierarchyTop (Read E) ===
#
# Spec §3.5 Read E (the load-bearing read): the τ-action under
# Read E IS the graded representation `graded_rep_RW : G → GL(⊕_N V_N)`
# of the family-root gauge group on the Russell-Whitehead-stratified
# universe. The species' ρ acts per-level by its level-N restriction;
# the action commutes with the inclusions V_N ↪ V_{N+1}.
#
# `graded_act(g, h)` is the τ-action under Read E: given a gauge-
# group element `g: ref` (inherited from coherence-parametric's
# lock_pair carrier; the same `g` autopoiesis's `gauge_act` operates
# on) and a hierarchy `h`, returns the graded-action-transformed
# hierarchy. The substrate's bundle tower exhibits this action at
# every level; THIS species names what that action IS at the
# bateson_learning altitude — the graded representation forced by
# Russell-Whitehead stratification.
#
# The canonicalness claim: graded_act is canonical because the
# species' explicit ancestry (Russell-Whitehead 1910 type
# stratification) names a standard mathematical structure (graded
# space), and that structure has a canonical representation of the
# gauge group (the graded rep) per Kac 1990 ch. 8. Same pattern
# as autopoiesis-as-adjoint per Seam §8.1.
#
# The bounded-commutator condition `[D, graded_act(g, ·)] = bounded
# shift operator` is automatic by the standard graded-algebra
# result (Kac 1990). The substrate-specific reading: at any finite
# stratification depth, the commutator is bounded; in the directed
# colimit (per recursion-locks.md §11.4), the condition holds by
# the standard limit construction.
#
# Body discharges per-species at the realisation boundary; the
# discharge IS the parametric carrier's lock_verdict applied with
# the species-specific graded representation.
graded_act(g: ref, h: hierarchy_top) -> hierarchy_top { \ }

# === learning_at_level — the level-N reading τ : bateson_system × level ↔ verdict ===
#
# The per-level reading at the species' altitude. Given a
# bateson_system and a target Russell-Whitehead level `n`, reads
# whether the system's regulation closes at level `n`. The verdict
# surfaces against the substrate's existing `transparency<level>`
# family per spec §6.7:
#
# - `success`           the regulation closes the Level N → Level
#                      N+1 lift gauge-equivalently to the graded
#                      representation.
# - `partial(opacity_map)`  the lift closes with recoverable loss;
#                          the opacity_map records which sub-N
#                          components of the lift failed
#                          gauge-equivalence.
# - `failure(opacity_map)`  the lift breaks; the system does not
#                          have bateson_learning at level `n`.
#
# Specialised per Bateson level by composition with the property
# sub-properties (per [[architecture-cybernetic-foundation]]
# §5.3):
#
# - learning_at_level(s, level_I)   IS the system's within-level
#                                    response change at the
#                                    Pavlovian-task altitude.
# - learning_at_level(s, level_II)  IS the system's context-of-
#                                    contexts generalisation per
#                                    Bateson 1964 (recognition
#                                    #41 cascade reading).
# - learning_at_level(s, level_III) IS the system's premises-of-
#                                    regulation change per
#                                    Bateson 1964 + Beer 1979
#                                    ch. 8 (task #271 algedonic
#                                    operational reading).
#
# Body discharges per-species at the realisation boundary; the
# discharge IS the level-N restriction of graded_act composed
# with the verdict-classification at the species boundary.
learning_at_level(s: bateson_system, n: level) -> verdict { \ }

# === bateson_well_formed — the bilateral inheritance predicate ===
#
# `bateson_well_formed(s)` is the predicate consumer shards name
# in a `requires` clause to declare bilateral agreement (per
# recognition #37, the Pask-agreement reading of `requires`) that
# the bateson_system carrier `s` they hold IS a well-formed
# Russell-Whitehead-Bateson stratified system — i.e., the
# hierarchy is graded by Russell-Whitehead level; the lift_map
# closes the Level N → Level N+1 transition gauge-equivalently
# to the graded representation; the species' three sub-properties
# (learning_I, learning_II, learning_III) hold at the levels
# the system inhabits.
#
# The bilateral commitment: the carrier commits to declaring the
# bateson_system five-tuple; the consumer commits to discharging
# the well-formedness verdict at the species boundary via the
# composition of:
#
# - @epistemologic/property/bateson_learning.bateson_learning(type)
#   — the declarative verifier with sub-properties learning_I /
#   learning_II / learning_III (inherited; not re-declared);
# - the five cybernetic-ancestor measurements at bateson_learning's
#   altitude (inherited from coherence-parametric; instantiated
#   with ρ = graded_rep_RW per spec §5);
# - the contraction-mode classification (bateson_learning is
#   static-with-grading; ω trivial; Russell-Whitehead lift
#   convergence by the standard graded-algebra recipe per Kac
#   1990 ch. 8).
#
# Declared as a typed predicate so downstream species shards can
# write `requires bateson_well_formed(my_system)` — same shape as
# `viable.viable_well_formed(v)`,
# `autopoiesis.autopoietic_closure_holds(s)`,
# `parametric_lock_witnessing(my_lock)`,
# `variety.variety_preserving(species)`,
# `second_order.second_order_well_formed(carrier)`, and the
# metalogue `requires substrate_pull_preserving(shim_X)` pattern
# at `shards/code/metalogue.mirror`. Body is `\`; the discharge
# IS the conjunction above at the species boundary.
#
# No `requires bateson_well_formed(…)` clauses are added at
# THIS shard. The property declares the predicate; consumers add
# the clauses when they pull. Per [[feedback-craft-not-deliver]]
# the operational sibling at `@mirror/cybernetics/bateson_learning`
# (and task #271 `@cyberpunk/algedonic`, which inherits this
# predicate via the Beer-altitude restriction) lands the same way.
bateson_well_formed(s: bateson_system) -> verdict { \ }

out @epistemologic/cybernetic/bateson_learning
out level
out hierarchy_top
out hierarchy_base
out bateson_system
out type_lift
out graded_act
out learning_at_level
out bateson_well_formed
